# Embedded Rust resource discipline

- Use idiomatic ownership, borrowing, explicit states, and bounded data first.
  Prefer compile-time work when it removes runtime cost; verify monomorphized
  code size before accepting a generic abstraction as cheap.
- Account separately for retained RAM, maximum live stack, heap allocations,
  copies, and code size. Fixed capacity and `no_std` do not prove low cost.
- Keep normal core/protocol paths allocation-free. Alloc-enabled paths still
  need explicit size bounds and a justified allocation lifetime.
- Borrow large buffers in place. Avoid profile-sized local scratch, whole-state
  rollback copies, and hidden collection conversions when a small view or
  deferred commit is sufficient.
- Refused operations must leave retryable protocol state intact. Validate and
  encode before committing progress; test failure followed by a successful retry.
- Treat short buffers, overflow, exhausted slots, cancellation, and stale
  completions as ordinary boundary cases, not panic paths.
- Minimize secret copies and use optimizer-resistant erasure on every exit.
- For resource-sensitive changes, inspect release output for the actual target,
  feature set, and pinned toolchain. Record comparable frame/symbol/image sizes.
  Static frames are not runtime stack high-water or device qualification.
- Optimize measured costs. Do not trade readable safe Rust for speculative
  unsafe code, forced inlining, or hand-written abstractions without evidence.
